<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sadeh">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sadeh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sadeh is an ancient Iranian festival celebrated with great bonfires fifty days before Nowruz, at the end of January, to honour fire and mark the waning of winter. Associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, it was celebrated lavishly at some medieval Muslim courts, notably by the Ziyarid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardavij">Mardavij</a> and the Ghaznavids, and it is still observed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sadeh.jpg" alt="Sadeh" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: دکتر علی نیکویی · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JashnSadeh1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…age, some 2100 verses or four percent of the whole, runs from Keyumars, the first man and first king, and his grandson Hushang, who discovered fire and founded the <b>Sadeh</b> Feast, through Tahmuras, Jamshid, Zahhak, Kaveh, and Fereydun and his three sons Salm, Tur and Iraj, to Manuchehr.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>He ruled from Isfahan, celebrated old Iranian festivals such as <b>Sadeh</b> and was said to plan the conquest of Baghdad and a revived Sasanian monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/ziyarid-dynasty">Ziyarid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
